  "query_preview": "..Visible satellite imagery shows the mid-upper level \ncloud deck that extended from about the I-40 corridor southward, is \nthinning from south to north. This cloud cover has likely delayed \nthe development of showers and thunderstorms along the higher \nterrain, especially closer to the I-40 corridor, to early this \nafternoon, while the White Mountains are currently starting to see \nshowers and thunderstorms develop. The latest sounding from \nFlagstaff shows decent instability with Mixed-layered CAPE around \n1100 J/KG and a little bit of shear, so we could see a handful of \nsevere storms similar to yesterday. Storm motion will likely be slow \n(around 5 mph) and some guidance suggesting a westward motion. This \ncould lead to a concern for training storms and lead to localized \nflash flooding.\n\nGuidance continues to support that the area of high pressure is \nprojected to settle over the Four Corners late this weekend or\nearly next week. This would allow more Monsoon moisture to lift\nnorthward and increase showers and thunderstorm coverage across\nnorthern Arizona. Prior to the incoming active monsoon days, we \ncould see a brief downward trend in convective activity Friday and\nSaturday. The NBM still has chances for showers/storms across the\nhigher elevation locations, but we are not anticipating more \nwidespread activity until Sunday onward. This pattern could also\nlead to an extension of the extreme heat warnings the Grand \nCanyon (below 4000 ft), Glen Canyon and Marble Canyon through at \nSunday.",
